    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Protein expression levels can determine disease genesis and progression. However, various processes occur between DNA and protein that can affect protein expression levels in the cell. Transcriptional allele-specific expression (ASE) is a process between DNA and RNA that yields differential expression of maternal and paternal alleles in the RNA. The mechanism behind this phenomenon has been a widely understood and applied to various cancer research initiatives. However, little work has been done to understand a similar process that happens between RNA and protein, translational allele-specific expression (TASE), where differential translational preference of alleles from mRNA contributes to differential expression of those alleles in proteins. Detecting TASE is more difficult because the same protein sequence could have come from various mRNAs and degradation makes it hard to quantify and sequence proteins accurately. Although few research papers have revealed the potential of TASE in yeast, none have shown this in humans. Here, we use a previously developed method to model and detect TASE in human samples. We develop a sensitive pipeline to quantify allelic expression in free mRNA and compare it to the quantity of those same alleles in mRNA that is actively being translated by ribosomes (‘ribosome-protected’ mRNA) of thirty human samples. We then use a bootstrapping method to detect the significant in the difference of allelic expression in these two groups of mRNA. We find that translational allele-specific expression occurs in almost all of the human samples, some alleles of which might be important for disease genesis.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
